      ":question/math-academy-id": "q-333568",
      ":question/problem": "The global truncation error (GTE) of a numerical method with step size $\u0394x$ is\n$e(\u0394x) = 7(\u0394x)^{3} - 2(\u0394x)^{4}$.\nComplete the following sentences. With step size $\u0394x = 0.6$, the global truncation error is $\\approx$\n{{field-1}}.\n\nWith step size $\u0394x = 0.4$, the global truncation error is $\\approx$\n{{field-2}}.\n\nDecreasing from $\u0394x = 0.6$ to $\u0394x = 0.4$, the GTE is reduced by a factor of $\\approx$\n{{field-3}}.\n\nAccording to this data, the order of the method is approximately\n{{field-4}}.\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question."
